WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:00.900
- All right everybody.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.310
We've reached the end of the hour,

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.500
but there's just enough time
for one more great story.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.409
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:05.409 --> 00:00:09.030
(rock music)
(audience cheering)

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.620
Spring is just around the corner

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.180
and that means our national parks

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.710
will soon be filled with visitors.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.480
I love national parks.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.280
A New Jersey teen wants to inspire

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.690
more people of color to visit

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.160
so she's written a children's book

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.600
called "Find Your Forest,"

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.790
that explores the magic
of the great outdoors

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.470
'cause it is very magical, y'all.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:26.670
We have her dialed in.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.830
Say hello to 16-year-old Audrey

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.872
who watches on NBC New York.

00:00:30.872 --> 00:00:31.869
Hi, Audrey.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:33.629
(audience cheering)
- Hi.

00:00:33.629 --> 00:00:35.640
- So Audrey, I'm with you.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.380
I love National Parks so much.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.810
I literally feel like I
live in one in Montana,

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.880
but what inspired you to do this?

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.213
- Yeah, so I grew up in Brooklyn, New York

00:00:44.213 --> 00:00:48.630
and living in Brooklyn it
really felt like forests

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:53.340
were some mystical, far away
place from where I lived.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.120
And when I got a little bit
older, we moved to New Jersey

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.920
and I was fortunate enough to
live very close to a forest.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.930
So, I spent a lot of time there

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.620
and really just realized
how incredible it is

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.420
to spend time in nature.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.500
It would calm me down and
allow me to clear my head.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.650
And so, I started doing
some research on this

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.530
and I discovered something
called the Nature Gap.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.090
And what this is is only about 10%

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.060
of National Park visitors
are people of color.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.190
And as a Latina myself,

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.890
this was something that really shocked me.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.130
So with this book, I
wanted to inspire kids

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:38.100
and families from all backgrounds

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.750
to get out into the forests.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.210
- The Earth, it's amazing, it's magical.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.443
And to be a part of it is amazing.

00:01:43.443 --> 00:01:46.290
So who did you work with to do this?

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.650
- Yeah, so I had the wonderful opportunity

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:53.404
to work with forest educators
at the US Forest Service,

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:57.090
along with a bilingual
children's media company

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.610
called Encantos and the Ad Council

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.740
who are the folks that
brought us Smokey the Bear.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:02.573
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:02.573 --> 00:02:05.220
- And with them I really
wanted to make sure

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.980
I was accurately portraying the animals,

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.980
plants, and insects that I
was demonstrating in the book.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.530
And not only did we want
this to be educational,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.060
but also fun.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.090
So, we have an activities
list in the back of the book

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.800
and one of my personal favorites

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.050
is something called sound mapping.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.720
And sound mapping is
when you close your eyes

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.570
and you listen to the
forest sounds around you

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.360
and you draw everything
out on a piece of paper.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.400
And with this book, I really hope

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.920
that I can go out into my local forest

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.630
or forests around the country

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.270
and see families of all colors

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.220
enjoying the forest as much as I do.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.087
- This is so cool, two
different languages right here,

00:02:46.087 --> 00:02:49.080
"Find Your Forest" is on
sale exclusively at Target.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.460
Y'all be sure to get a copy.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.510
Thank you so much, Audrey.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.068
I'm liking your post right now, all right.

00:02:53.068 --> 00:02:54.760
(audience cheering)
- [Audrey] Thank you.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.310
- And that's not all, ma'am.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.110
Our season-long partner, Pilot Pen,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.790
makers of FriXion, the go-to erasable pen

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:00.750
of "The Kelly Clarkson Show"

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.700
wanna celebrate those creating fearlessly

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:03.600
just like you, ma'am,

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.292
so they're gonna give you
$1,000 just for being rad.

00:03:06.292 --> 00:03:09.928
(audience cheering)

00:03:09.928 --> 00:03:12.095
Thank you so much, Audrey.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.787
Thank you to all our guests this hour,

00:03:14.787 --> 00:03:17.720
Sir Rod Stewart, Jools
Holland, Camila Mendes,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.620
Chef Scott, and Melissa.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.923
Y'all have a great day,
and if it's not, change it.

